[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver_multi/tests/semver_range_impl_test.py: The parse_range/satisfies contract is only partially exercised, leaving several contracted grammar constructs and postconditions unverified. Caret expansion is never exercised for left-most non-zero components below the major: a zero-major range with a non-zero minor and a zero-major range with a zero minor must expand to distinct exclusive upper boundaries. The greater-than relational comparator from the supported comparator set is never evaluated for satisfaction, so no scenario shows a candidate above a '>' boundary matching while a candidate below it does not. A version token that violates the semantic-version grammar inside any comparator or hyphen expression must raise InvalidRangeError instead of propagating the version parser's failure. Complete versions carrying build metadata are never parsed or matched in a range and build metadata must be ignored for precedence, while a version token supplying '+' with no build identifiers must raise InvalidRangeError as malformed syntax. The prefix and relational comparator forms applied to wildcard partials are untested: caret applied to wildcard partials with zero and non-zero majors, and >=, >, <, <=, and != applied to wildcard bands, including relational operators applied to a bare full wildcard, which must be accepted and must match no version while the unbounded >= and <= wildcard forms match every version. Add public parse_range/satisfies scenarios covering these contracted constructs and their postconditions.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver_multi/lib/semver_range_impl.py: The clause compiler contains two statements that no conforming public call sequence can reach under the range parsing and satisfaction contract, so they must be pruned or annotated with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: <reason>)'. The 'return []' at line 162 in the hyphen-range branch is unreachable because hyphen operands are only ever expanded with the '>=' and '<=' operators, whose complete-version and wildcard expansions always yield at least one clause alternative, so the empty-alternative guard can never fire; remove it rather than retaining dead defensive code. The 'break' at line 190 in the clause tokenizer is likewise unreachable because every clause reaching the tokenizer has already had its surrounding whitespace trimmed by the range compiler, so the whitespace scan always stops at a non-whitespace character and the loop terminates through its own condition before the end-of-input break can be taken. Remove these two statements or mark them unreachable with the stated caller assumption so full statement coverage is achievable without asserting impossible caller behavior.
